# What I Explored

## 1. TCP SYN Flooding

A SYN flood targets the TCP three-way handshake by generating large numbers of SYN requests without completing normal connections.

A normal TCP connection follows this process:

```text
Client → SYN
Server → SYN-ACK
Client → ACK
```

During a SYN flood, the target receives repeated SYN requests without the connection being completed. This can force the system to maintain large numbers of incomplete or half-open connections.

During the lab, Wireshark displayed repeated TCP SYN traffic directed toward the Windows target.

### Visual Representation

![SYN Flood Wireshark Capture](images/syn-flood-wireshark.png)

*Wireshark displaying repeated TCP SYN packets directed toward the Windows target during the controlled lab simulation.*

The packet capture made the attack pattern much easier to recognize because the same type of connection request appeared continuously against the same target.

### Defensive Meaning

A large volume of incomplete TCP connection attempts is an important indicator of SYN-flood activity. A defender may look for:

* Large numbers of SYN packets
* Incomplete TCP handshakes
* Repeated connections toward the same destination
* Abnormal connection-table growth
* Sudden changes in packets per second

This demonstrated that even very small packets can affect availability when enough of them force a system to continuously allocate connection resources.

