## 4. Large ICMP Packet Demonstration

The lab also included a large-packet ICMP exercise.

A Windows system repeatedly sent **65,500-byte ICMP packets** to the target.

### Visual Representation

![Large ICMP Packet Demonstration](images/large-icmp.png)

*Continuous 65,500-byte ICMP traffic used to observe how the target handled unusually large packets in the controlled environment.*

The target continued responding during the exercise.

Instead of crashing, the system remained operational while measurable network activity was generated.

### What I Learned

The exercise demonstrated the concept behind the historical **Ping of Death** attack.

Modern operating systems are generally better at safely handling oversized or malformed packets, so the target remaining responsive was an important observation.

The value of the exercise was understanding how unusually large packets appear on the network and how packet-handling weaknesses could historically affect system stability.

### Defensive Meaning

Organizations should:

* Keep operating systems patched
* Maintain current network-device firmware
* Monitor for abnormal packet sizes
* Use IDS/IPS protections
* Apply rate limiting and filtering where appropriate

