## 6. Metasploit SYN Flood Simulation

The final portion of the lab used the **Metasploit Framework** to demonstrate SYN-flood behavior.

The lab used the following Metasploit module:

```text
auxiliary/dos/tcp/synflood
```

The simulation targeted the assigned Windows lab system.

### Visual Representation

![Metasploit SYN Flood](images/metasploit-syn-flood.png)

*Metasploit Framework running the SYN-flood module against the authorized Windows lab target.*

The module output showed the SYN flooding activity directed toward the designated system and service.

### What I Learned

This exercise demonstrated the same general attack technique through a different tool.

Earlier in the lab, `hping3` generated SYN traffic directly. Metasploit later performed a similar task through a framework module.

That helped reinforce an important lesson:

> The attack technique matters more than the tool used to generate it.

A defender should focus on identifying the behavior rather than trying to detect only one specific application.

### Defensive Meaning

Whether the traffic comes from `hping3`, Metasploit, or another source, defenders may still observe:

* Repeated SYN packets
* High connection rates
* Incomplete handshakes
* Traffic spikes
* Service degradation

